<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Two tracks, one room</h2>

    <div class="track track-a">
      <h3>Track A — Foundations</h3>
      <p class="who">For those beginning their careers.</p>
      <p>
        You learn what an AI agent actually is. What today's models can do,
        what they can't, where they guess, why they break.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then you build. Small agents that do one real thing — something a
        person in this town would actually use. Not a demo you show once and
        delete.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also learn to read the bigger system we're building: trace it,
        find the failure before anyone announces it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="track track-b">
      <h3>Track B — Build</h3>
      <p class="who">For those three to five years in.</p>
      <p>You own a piece of the town agent and you ship it.</p>
      <p>
        You'll hit the problems tutorials don't have. A shop with no written
        process. An owner whose pricing follows no rule. A model that sounds
        certain and is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's a session where I stay silent and you explain your piece to
        the room.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="together">
      Same room, same system. Track A reads what Track B builds. Track B
      explains it. Neither works without the other.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

